{% block content %}
<p>Tests that call a real LLM are slow, cost money and give different answers each run.
Almost everything here can be tested without one.</p>

<h2>Layer 1: pure Python</h2>
<p>The world model, dice, commands and the tools themselves are ordinary functions. The
tool tests call them directly, injecting <code>ToolContext</code> by hand. Run them with:</p>
<pre><code>cd examples/adventure
uv run --extra dev pytest</code></pre>

<h2>Layer 2: a scripted fake LLM</h2>
<p><code>LLMInteraction</code> accepts a pre-built client. We hand it a fake one that
returns scripted tool calls, shaped exactly like Responses API output:</p>
{{ snippet("../tests/conftest.py", "fake_client") }}

<div class="box lib"><code>LLMInteraction(prompt_dir=..., client=my_client, model="...")</code>
skips all authentication and wraps any OpenAI-compatible client. In tests, "compatible"
just means "has <code>.responses.create()</code>".</div>

<p>Now a whole turn runs through the <em>real</em> agent loop, the <em>real</em> tools and
the <em>real</em> save file &mdash; only the model's decisions are scripted:</p>
{{ snippet("../tests/test_turn.py", "test_invent_and_save") }}

